    "answer": {
      "short": "$g(f(x))=-x-1$ for $x\\geq0$. The expression $f(g(x))=\\sqrt{-x^2-1}$ is undefined over the real numbers because $-x^2-1<0$ for every real $x$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}g(f(x))&=-(\\sqrt{x})^2-1\\\\&=-x-1,\\qquad x\\geq0.\\end{aligned}$$\n$$\\begin{aligned}f(g(x))&=\\sqrt{-x^2-1}.\\end{aligned}$$\nFor every real $x$, $x^2\\geq0$, so $-x^2-1\\leq-1<0$. Therefore $f(g(x))$ has no real values and does not exist as a real-valued composite function.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
